---
import LessonShell from '../../layouts/LessonShell.astro';
---
<LessonShell
    course="LolRust"
    number={1}
    name="Your First Meow"
    objective="Make the cat say something. Anything. The litter box is yours."
    keywords={[
        { code: 'iz main()', note: 'every program starts here' },
        { code: 'meow!(...)', note: 'make the cat speak' },
        { code: '"..."', note: 'wrap text in quotes' },
        { code: ';', note: 'end every statement' },
    ]}
    initial={`iz main() {
    // TODO: make the cat say something
    // hint: meow!("...your message...");

}`}
    checks={[
        { label: 'has fn main', pattern: '\\bfn\\s+main\\s*\\(' },
        { label: 'has println!', pattern: '\\bprintln!\\s*\\(' },
        { label: 'no leftover meow keywords', pattern: '\\b(iz|meow!)\\b', negate: true },
    ]}
    next={{ href: '/lolrust/lesson-2', label: 'lesson 2' }}
    hints={[
        'Every lolrust program needs an entry point. Type `iz main()` followed by curly braces.',
        'Inside the braces, use `meow!` (with the `!`) and put your message inside `"..."` quotes.',
        'Don\'t forget the semicolon `;` at the end of the line.',
    ]}
    solution={`iz main() {
    meow!("hai hooman! i am learning lolrust!");
}`}
>
    <div slot="extras">
        <h3>How the checker works</h3>
        <p class="dim">
            The transpiler runs in your browser via WebAssembly. Hit
            <strong>Run</strong> and you see the actual Rust your code became.
            The verdict checks structural properties of that Rust ~ it's not
            faking success.
        </p>
    </div>
</LessonShell>
